{
  "passage": "Ashoka was one of the most famous rulers of ancient India. He belonged to the Mauryan dynasty, which was founded by his grandfather, Chandragupta Maurya, with the help of a wise minister named Chanakya. Ashoka became king in about 268 BCE and ruled a vast empire with its capital at Pataliputra, near present-day Patna. About eight years after he became king, Ashoka fought a war to conquer Kalinga, in present-day Odisha. The war was terrible. Thousands of people were killed and many more were taken captive. Ashoka was so filled with sorrow at the suffering he saw that he decided never to fight a war of conquest again. He turned to the teachings of the Buddha and began to follow what he called dhamma. Ashoka's dhamma was not a new religion. It asked people to be kind to animals, to respect their elders and teachers, to treat servants and slaves well, and to respect the religions of others. To spread these ideas, Ashoka had his messages carved on rocks and on polished stone pillars across his empire. Most of these inscriptions were written in the Prakrit language using the Brahmi script. The lions on top of his pillar at Sarnath form the national emblem of India today, and the wheel on it appears on our national flag.",
